The common-words generator sizes its context by binary search over the number of distinct words, with an
upper bound of twice its estimate. Above about 4M tokens that bound passes the 370101 words of
json/english_words.json and random.sample raises before the search can descend. The bound is clamped to
the list - every size the search actually tests is unchanged.

Every generator sizes a prompt by tokenizing all of it, at every step of its search and for every sample -
about fifty full tokenizations of a 10M-token prompt per cell. The HF tokenizer's count is now taken in
pieces: the prompt is cut at the spaces that follow a non-space, which GLM's pre-tokenizer never joins to
the character before (no alternative of its pattern matches a non-space then a space, and none looks
behind), the pieces are tokenized in batches on every core, and a piece seen before in the process - the
haystack each step and sample repeats - is not tokenized again. The count is the one a single tokenization
gives, so every comparison, prompt and sample is unchanged. Any other tokenizer, or a text holding a special
token, is counted whole as before.

